    r2t:"EU Battery Passport requirements and performance testing and verification",
    r2w:"Kongju National University / HUBA · BME",
    r2p1:"The EU Battery Regulation specifies what must be disclosed, but not how it should be measured or evidenced. This work designs a performance testing and verification framework that links each performance claim to an identified battery, its measurement conditions, and a review record.",
    r2h1:"Battery categories and the scope of the passport",
    r2p2:"From 18 February 2027, Article 77(1) of the Regulation applies the passport to each EV battery, each light means of transport (LMT) battery, and each industrial battery exceeding 2 kWh placed on the market. The 2 kWh threshold qualifies industrial batteries only; classification follows the Article 3 definitions and the intended application.",
    r2l1:"<b>Specimen versus marketed product</b> \u2014 a module removed from a vehicle for testing does not automatically become a separately marketed product requiring its own passport. The intake record should distinguish the specimen, the marketed product, and their identifiers",
    r2l2:"<b>Reclassification on repurposing</b> \u2014 repurposing an EV battery for stationary storage can change its category and its performance and safety requirements, so the transformation must be documented and the resulting product assessed",
    r2l3:"<b>QR code versus passport</b> \u2014 Article 13(6) QR information access applies to all batteries, while passport coverage is narrower. A successful QR scan alone does not establish regulatory readiness",
    r2h2:"Application dates",
    r2c1:"Date", r2c2:"Obligation",
    r2d1:"Regulation enters into force", r2d2:"General application (subject to transition provisions)",
    r2d3:"Battery management system (BMS) information requirements apply", r2d4:"Battery passport mandatory for covered batteries",
    r2d5:"Supply-chain due diligence applies (postponed two years by Regulation (EU) 2025/1561)",
    r2p3:"Carbon-footprint and recycled-content requirements follow a different schedule, combining fixed dates with intervals after relevant secondary legislation enters into force. A date in a summary timetable should not be read as the whole condition.",
    r2h3:"Verification architecture",
    r2p4:"Three kinds of records share an object reference and are managed separately, so that an approved result stays linked to its evidence without the underlying records being made public.",
    r2l4:"<b>Laboratory record</b> \u2014 observations, instrument information, and custody events",
    r2l5:"<b>Assessment record</b> \u2014 calculations, uncertainty, exceptions, and conclusions",
    r2l6:"<b>Exchange record</b> \u2014 approved values, definitions, and authorized evidence references",
    r2h4:"Operator responsibility and the role of KHBCC",
    r2p5:"Under Article 77(4), the economic operator placing a battery on the market is responsible for accurate and current passport information. Under Article 77(7), a battery that has been prepared for re-use, repurposed, or remanufactured requires a new passport linked to the original.",
    r2call:"<b>KHBCC is not a battery passport issuer.</b> Research participation confers neither a manufacturer's responsibility nor a notified body's authority. KHBCC supports the production and exchange of evidence as a testing and verification data hub, focusing on data quality, methodology, and inter-laboratory comparison.",
    refsT:"References", refBase:"2025/26 EIPP final report, Chapter 3. Korea Development Institute and Yonsei University (2026).",
    rpEyebrow:"Reports", rpTitle:"Policy consultation reports",
    rpLede:"KHBCC grew out of a four-year policy consultation project under the Korea\u2013Hungary Economic Innovation Partnership Program (EIPP). Each year's final report is available on the KDI EIPP website.",
    rpSearch:"Search the EIPP publication list for <code>Korea-Hungary</code> to see this project's reports together.",
